	<!-- Piers: Arabic typefaces. The three fontset ids stay as upstream Bald has them (Default, InstrumentSans,
	     Arial) so Appearance > Typography, scripts/info.py and Bald Helper keep working; only the files change:
	     Default = Inter Mohand, InstrumentSans = Inter iPhone, Arial = Inter Tonos. Each has Regular and Bold only, so
	     Regular/Medium map to Regular and SemiBold/Bold to Bold. Linespacing values are rescaled by each face's
	     Kodi cell height (max(head.yMax, ascender) - min(head.yMin, descender)) so multi-line text keeps upstream's pixel line height. -->
	<!-- DM Sans, the default typeface: its id is "Default", lookandfeel.font's default value in Kodi, so a fresh install
	     and a skin switch land here (an earlier "DMSans" choice, no longer an id, falls back here too); the label comes
	     from idloc. It is the Instrument Sans set with each weight swapped for the same DM Sans weight (static opsz 14
	     cuts). Names and sizes stay identical to InstrumentSans. DM Sans' natural line is taller (1.302 em against
	     1.22), so every font drawn on several lines (textboxes, wrapped labels) has its own linespacing here that
	     brings Kodi's pixel line height back to Instrument Sans' within 0.5 px (tests/test_fonts.py). -->
	<fontset id="Default" idloc="31941">
		<!-- Estuary names kept for the copied utility windows; font13 also stays because Kodi falls back to it for any
		     font name a window asks for that the skin lacks. font23_narrow and font32 use Kodi's bundled arial.ttf in
		     every fontset: they only draw text from outside the skin (a game add-on's emulator name and version in
		     DialogAddonSettings, the screen calibration readout), which arial.ttf's wide glyph coverage suits. -->
		<font>
			<name>font12</name>
			<filename>Mohand-Regular.ttf</filename>
			<size>25</size>
			<linespacing>0.895</linespacing>
